<p>Mexican authorities say the army killed Nemesio Rubén Oseguera Cervantes — “El Mencho,” the longtime leader of the Jalisco New Generation Cartel (CJNG) — during an operation in the western state of Jalisco on Sunday. Officials said he was wounded in the clash and later died. Multiple outlets describe the CJNG as one of Mexico’s most powerful and violent criminal organizations, and U.S. authorities had pursued Oseguera for years. After the operation, authorities and news reports said cartel-linked reprisals flared across multiple states. Reports described road blockades, torched vehicles, and attacks that disrupted daily life — including canceled classes in some areas and official warnings urging residents and visitors to avoid affected zones. Airlines and governments also issued safety guidance as disruptions hit travel in parts of western Mexico, including around tourist hubs such as Puerto Vallarta and Guadalajara, where some flights were canceled. U.S. officials and some reports said the United States provided intelligence support for the operation, highlighting ongoing security cooperation as Mexico faces pressure to curb cartel power and cross-border drug trafficking. While El Mencho’s death removes a central figure from the CJNG, reporting also points to a familiar risk: succession fights and splintering can fuel fresh instability, keeping public safety and rule-of-law efforts in sharp focus.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/kw0z9S03HzcrwV-AWSWW3Htj9OyATCkRTsnFvS-dYuC6HhRpQ4DWuntfxdbzYReE2ZOlbqTQHlFAMdmk5NpxUbZ-PocKyAwaTWcl9t043YGv5dCch8pwoAkaGphV2Zix1OtgNg-QbEGCOtMrxcnhfakSf_R53pO5k13ql8xbfinVQzY4hRNaT2_IR_Nd7d-yCqJzMdSFGVa2mXCZ-jhg4NEnc_-hcwfbSFzV1wuuLhvqc0puZbmZdYhfXzVT0RSZxhlWDSynli6ayNLRAw' alt='A soldier stands guard by a charred vehicle after it was set on fire in Michoacán state following reports of reprisals after El Mencho’s death.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Where it happened: Mexico’s defense ministry said the operation took place near Tapalpa in Jalisco state. Reports said Oseguera was wounded in the clash and later died while being transported.</li><li>Reprisals reported: In the wake of the federal operation, officials and outlets described “narco-blockades” — burning vehicles used to cut off roads — along with other attacks. Reports said the violence spread beyond Jalisco, with the BBC describing retaliatory incidents in at least a dozen states.</li><li>Travel disruptions: Travel alerts and airline updates cited cancellations and disruptions at airports including Puerto Vallarta and Guadalajara. Reporting also flagged other affected routes, such as Manzanillo, as carriers adjusted schedules amid unrest.</li><li>CJNG footprint: Background reporting said the CJNG expanded beyond drug trafficking into other rackets such as stolen fuel and human trafficking, helping drive its national reach.</li><li>U.S. support: Reuters-sourced reporting said a new U.S. military-led intelligence-collection task force focused on mapping cartel networks helped Mexico’s hunt, alongside U.S.-Mexico information-sharing highlighted by officials after the raid.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House (US): The White House said the United States provided “intelligence support” to Mexico and described El Mencho as a top target for both governments. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/white-house-confirms-providing-intelligence-support-to-mexican-govt-for-operation-that-killed-cartel-leader-el-mencho/article70665679.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Jalisco state government: Jalisco Governor Pablo Lemus Navarro said multiple vehicles were set on fire across the state after the federal security operation and urged caution as authorities responded. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/vehicles-torched-in-mexicos-jalisco-following-federal-operation'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: The U.S. State Department urged Americans in parts of Mexico affected by the violence to 